    "result_text": "I've implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py` and added tests. All 8 tests pass (`pytest -q`).\n\n**What it does:**\n- **Retries:** it runs `iter_answer` again from the start, up to `ATTEMPTS` (3) times. Each attempt's text is only joined into a string once the stream finishes, so text from a dropped attempt is never returned. The history is copied into a list first, so every attempt sends the same messages even if the caller passed something that can only be read once.\n- **Warning log:** the SDK doesn't pass the network error's message through. Both a failed connection and a drop mid-stream come out as `APIConnectionError` with just the text \"Connection error.\", and the real `httpx2` error is attached as its cause. So the warning logs the cause's message instead, for example `Attempt 1/3 to reach the model failed: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`. Logging the SDK's own message would have told ops nothing.\n- **All attempts fail:** it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, with the last connection error attached as its cause.\n- **Timeouts:** in the SDK, `APITimeoutError` is a subtype of `APIConnectionError`, so a plain \"retry on connection error\" check would also retry timeouts. I check for the timeout first, so a timeout raises `UpstreamTimeout` straight away, whether it happens while connecting or mid-stream.\n\nThe client is used as given and no dependencies were added.\n\n**New tests** (`tests/test_chat.py`) use the SDK's real exception types, chained from `httpx2` errors the same way the SDK does it. They cover:\n- a normal answer;\n- a drop mid-stream followed by a clean retry, checking that only the complete text comes back, the same messages are resent, and the warning contains the network error's message;\n- a failed connection followed by success;\n- all three attempts failing, which raises `UpstreamUnavailable` and logs three warnings;\n- a timeout while connecting and mid-stream, each raising `UpstreamTimeout` after a single attempt.\n\nThe client's "
  },
